From: Alan Rockefeller <alanrockefeller@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 9 Oct 2026 06:02:56 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 5/7] AVX 4x4 sumtable (site repeats): matrix-vector products
 from columns

corax_core_update_sumtable_repeatsbclv_4x4_avx() (~11% of a run) built both
terms of every sumtable entry as row products plus a 4x4 transpose-add. It
now uses the column form with the zero-accumulator variant -- the original
adds every product to a zeroed vector, which turns a -0 product into +0, and
adding +0 once to the finished sum gives the same bits
(canon(a)+canon(b) == canon(a+b) for all doubles) -- and two sites per
iteration share the column loads. The small per-call tables (transposed
inverse eigenvectors here, the per-rate pointer arrays in
sumtable_repeats()) are on the stack instead of five heap allocations per
call.

Harness: 4000 random cases (eigenvectors with negative and zero entries,
inv on/off, per-rate and per-site scalers, child with and without repeats):
all identical. 1.69x faster per call (218 sites, 4 categories).

Co-authored-by: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
